Let P=F[z_1,…,z_d] and K=F(z_1,…,z_d), with S_r the automorphism swapping z_r,z_{r+1}, δ_r=z_r−z_{r+1}. Let z_j act by multiplication and let the algebra generator s_r act by A_r=S_r+(S_r−1)/δ_r=(1+1/δ_r)S_r−1/δ_r. The operator preserves P: S_rp−p is divisible by δ_r, over every field. The following verification in the crossed product K⋊S_d proves the defining relations even on K.

Mixed relations: A_r z_r−z_{r+1}A_r=−1 and A_r z_{r+1}−z_r A_r=1 by direct substitution; A_r commutes with other z_j. Set a(t)=1+1/t and b(t)=−1/t, so A_r=a(δ_r)S_r+b(δ_r). Since S_r(δ_r)=−δ_r, the identity coefficient of A_r² is a(δ_r)a(−δ_r)+b(δ_r)²=1 and its S_r coefficient is a(δ_r)b(−δ_r)+b(δ_r)a(δ_r)=0. Thus A_r²=1. Distant A_r commute, because their permutations and coefficients involve disjoint variable pairs.

For the adjacent braid write u=z_r−z_{r+1}, v=z_{r+1}−z_{r+2}, w=u+v=z_r−z_{r+2}, and S=S_r,T=S_{r+1}. Use (fσ)(gτ)=fσ(g)στ. Expanding either A_r A_{r+1} A_r or A_{r+1} A_r A_{r+1} gives the following six permutation coefficients, respectively:
identity: −(uv+1)/(uvw);
S: a(u)/(vw);
T: a(v)/(uw);
ST: −a(u)a(w)/v;
TS: −a(v)a(w)/u;
STS=TST: a(u)a(w)a(v).
For example the left S coefficient is a(u)[b(w)b(−u)+b(u)b(v)]=a(u)/(vw), the left T coefficient is b(u)a(v)b(w)=a(v)/(uw), and its identity coefficient is b(u)²b(v)+a(u)b(w)a(−u)=−(uv+1)/(uvw). Interchanging u,v and S,T gives the right expansion with the same six coefficients. All are rational identities valid in every characteristic, including two. Hence the braid holds. Thus the presentation defines a homomorphism into operators on K (and P).

Spanning: use s_r p=(S_rp)s_r−(p−S_rp)/(z_r−z_{r+1}) to move every polynomial left. Coxeter relations give the symmetric-group standard words w, so the ordered monomials z_1^{a_1}⋯z_d^{a_d}w span, with a_j≥0. This uses only the finite symmetric-group presentation/standard-word reduction.

Independence: for a reduced word w=S_{i_1}⋯S_{i_m}, the operator A_{i_1}⋯A_{i_m} has coefficient c_w=∏_{k=1}^m(1+1/(S_{i_1}⋯S_{i_{k−1}})(δ_{i_k})) at permutation w. Each factor is a nonzero rational function, so c_w≠0 over any F. Every other expansion term uses a proper subword of a length-m reduced expression, so its permutation length is strictly below m. Therefore, in a finite relation Σ_w p_w(z)A_w=0, choose the largest length with p_w≠0. The coefficient at each such permutation w is p_w c_w, hence cannot vanish in K.

To justify that a zero operator has zero crossed-product coefficients, prove finite independence of distinct field automorphisms: if Σ_j f_j σ_j=0 is a nontrivial operator relation with minimum number of nonzero terms, normalize one coefficient to one. For any t∈K, subtract σ_1(t) times this relation from its composition with multiplication by t, obtaining Σ_{j≠1}f_j(σ_j(t)−σ_1(t))σ_j=0. Distinct σ_j and σ_1 disagree on some t, so this is a nontrivial shorter relation, contradiction. A one-term relation cannot annihilate 1. This finite argument needs no choice. Hence the displayed coefficient argument proves independence. After renaming z_j=x_j, the ordered monomials in the degenerate affine Hecke presentation form an F-basis. No cyclotomic quotient, BK isomorphism, or dimension theorem is used; d=0 has singleton empty monomial.

